nBoth KServe and Dashboard exist in `.context/architecture-context/`\n(rhoai-3.0) and are Konflux-onboarded — no onboarding chain (Rule 20) is\nneeded. No new components are provisioned.\n\nComponent mapping notes:\n- The common audit event schema epic is assigned canonical component\n  `KServe` (single most-relevant name): the schema is authored by the\n  serving team whose component emits the first conforming events, and there\n  is no canonical \"platform-wide schema\" component. The deliverable is\n  explicitly component-agnostic per HLR-4.\n- HLR-5 requires documentation, but `Documentation` is not listed in the\n  strategy's Affected Components. It is added as an epic under the canonical\n  `Documentation` component.\n\n## Step 3 — Investigations\n\nThe strategy declares no open questions, no pending reviews, and no\nconditional ADRs; the audit sink interface is stated to be the standard\ncluster logging forwarder. No unknown changes which epics exist or what they\ndo. The only sequencing constraint (HLR-4's note that HLR-1 cannot ship\nbefore the schema is settled) is a build-against artifact relationship\n(Rule 13), not an uncertainty — it is modeled as a dependency edge, not an\nInvestigation. The strategy is compliance-driven but is not a\nguardrails/sandboxing/RBAC enforcement strategy (Rule 19 does not trigger).\n\n## Step 4 — HLR-to-epic mapping\n\n- HLR-1 (P0) and HLR-2 (P0) both describe the KServe audit event pipeline —\n  emission at the inference endpoint and reliable at-least-once delivery of\n  those same events. Same component, same team, same logical change over the\n  same event stream: one Implementation epic covering both HLRs at P0.\n- HLR-3 (P1) is a distinct, deferrable feature on a different component and\n  UI surface (Dashboard per-project control): separate epic at P1.\n- HLR-4 (P2) is the published common audit event schema. It is a distinct\n  deliverable (a published contract for future components) that HLR-1's\n  events must conform to, so it is its own epic. Because it transitively\n  gates all P0 work (HLR-1/HLR-2), it inherits P0 (priority inheritance).\n- HLR-5 (P2) is substantive documentation authoring (new doc topic), a\n  separate work-product type: separate `docs-authoring` epic. Per the\n  docs-authoring priority exception, its priority derives from the strategy\n  frontmatter priority (Critical → P0), and its edges do not trigger\n  priority inheritance.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 | Define and publish the common inference audit event schema | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 | KServe inference audit event emission and at-least-once delivery | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 | Dashboard per-project control for inference auditing | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 | Document enabling inference auditing and interpreting audit events | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P0 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Common audit event schema\"] --> E002[\"E002 KServe emission + delivery\"]\n    E002 --> E003[\"E003 Dashboard per-project control\"]\n    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Documentation\"]\n    E002 --> E004\n    E003 --> E004\n```\n\nCritical path: E001 → E002 → E003 → E004 (length 4).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The schema is the format HLR-1's events must conform to; the strategy explicitly states HLR-1 cannot ship before the schema is settled. The emission implementation builds against the published schema. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The Dashboard control writes the per-project audit enablement configuration that the serving-side implementation defines and honors. Per architecture context, the Dashboard owns no data plane and reads/writes CRs — it builds against the config surface produced by the KServe-side work. |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must describe the published schema as part of interpreting events. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built (emission and delivery behavior). |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers enabling auditing per project from the Dashboard. |\n\nNotes:\n- No `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics:\n  both target components already exist in the platform and are Konflux\n  onboarded per architecture context.\n- No Investigation epics: no open questions, pending reviews, or conditional\n  ADRs; the audit sink interface is stated as the standard cluster logging\n  forwarder.\n- docs-authoring edges (→ E004) do not trigger priority inheritance, so E003\n  remains P1 despite blocking the P0 docs epic.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|---|---|---|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 |\n| HLR-5 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 |\n\nAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-3's serving-side enforcement\n(the per-project enablement setting the serving stack honors) is scope\nwithin E002; its user-facing surface is E003.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** no Impacted Teams table, no\n  Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n  Non-Functional Requirements. Owner teams were derived from architecture\n  context; epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n- **Priority inversion:** HLR-4 is labeled P2 yet explicitly gates the P0\n  HLR-1 (\"HLR-1 cannot ship before the schema is settled\"). Mitigated in the\n  decomposition by priority inheritance: the schema epic (E001) is P0.\n- **Scope trap (durable delivery):** HLR-2's \"not lost on component\n  restart\" plus at-least-once semantics implies durable buffering in the\n  serving delivery path — more than a simple logging hook. The strategy does\n  not bound this; verify the intended durability mechanism during\n  implementation planning.\n- **Schema ownership tension:** HLR-4's schema must serve future components\n  (pipelines, workbenches) but is being defined under the KServe component\n  by the serving team. Reviewers should confirm the schema is genuinely\n  component-agnostic and its publication location is agreed platform-wide.\n- **Documentation component absent from Affected Components:** HLR-5\n  requires documentation; the strategy lists only KServe and Dashboard. A\n  Documentation epic was added — confirm the intended docs owner.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing; all work is\nin-platform and linearly gated by the schema.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":27137,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9237710a8f900247","agent_id":"a76fe8965019b7c94","session_id":"d259959c-9f88-4e8b-bf9c-c556eba940a6","uuid":"2c8c6d6a-271d-446d-a489-f78826633980","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:44:07.541Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9010"}
