{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-f72f14de-dd23-43b7-b5fb-5288e67c9df9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9585ec29bc1d2626","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9010-ropnvl1h/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9010-E001.md","content":"# Define and publish the common inference audit event schema\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the common audit event schema for model inference\ntraffic: a versioned, published definition of the structured audit event\nthat inference components emit. The schema captures caller identity,\nmodel/version, timestamp, and response status, and explicitly excludes\nrequest/response payloads. It is the contract that inference audit event\nemission conforms to and that future components (pipelines, workbenches)\nwill adopt in later strategies, so it must be component-agnostic rather than\nspecific to any single emitter.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define the audit event schema: fields for caller identity, model and\n  model version, timestamp, and response status (success/failure outcome).\n- State explicitly in the schema that request and response payloads are not\n  captured, so emitters and auditors have an unambiguous data-minimization\n  contract.\n- Version the schema so future components can emit compatible events and\n  evolution is possible without breaking consumers.\n- Publish the schema at a location accessible to platform components and\n  auditors, suitable for adoption by future strategies (pipelines,\n  workbenches are out of scope for this strategy but named as adopters).\n- Align the delivery target semantics with the cluster audit sink (the\n  standard cluster logging forwarder interface) so events defined by this\n  schema can be forwarded without translation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The schema document exists and defines fields covering caller identity,\n  model/version, timestamp, and response status.\n- The schema states that payloads are excluded from audit events.\n- The schema carries a version identifier and a documented compatibility\n  expectation for future emitting components.\n- The schema is published at an agreed, discoverable location reachable by\n  platform components and by consumers of the cluster audit sink.\n- The schema has been reviewed for component-agnosticism: nothing in it\n  presumes a single emitter's internal structure.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-4 (P2): A common audit event schema is defined and published so\n  future components emit compatible events. This epic also produces the\n  format that the P0 inference audit events (HLR-1) must conform to, which\n  is why this epic is prioritized P0 despite HLR-4's P2 label.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":29635,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9237710a8f900247","agent_id":"a76fe8965019b7c94","session_id":"d259959c-9f88-4e8b-bf9c-c556eba940a6","uuid":"4968525b-a60b-41f2-9d38-9a15ac0c1a01","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:44:27.613Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9010"}
