{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aaa034b8389fc4b5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9010-E001\n3\ttitle: Define and publish the common inference audit event schema\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n5\tcomponent: KServe\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: -1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 0\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 3\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Define and publish the common inference audit event schema\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic delivers the common audit event schema for model inference\n34\ttraffic: a versioned, published definition of the structured audit event\n35\tthat inference components emit. The schema captures caller identity,\n36\tmodel/version, timestamp, and response status, and explicitly excludes\n37\trequest/response payloads. It is the contract that inference audit event\n38\temission conforms to and that future components (pipelines, workbenches)\n39\twill adopt in later strategies, so it must be component-agnostic rather than\n40\tspecific to any single emitter.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Define the audit event schema: fields for caller identity, model and\n45\t  model version, timestamp, and response status (success/failure outcome).\n46\t- State explicitly in the schema that request and response payloads are not\n47\t  captured, so emitters and auditors have an unambiguous data-minimization\n48\t  contract.\n49\t- Version the schema so future components can emit compatible events and\n50\t  evolution is possible without breaking consumers.\n51\t- Publish the schema at a location accessible to platform components and\n52\t  auditors, suitable for adoption by future strategies (pipelines,\n53\t  workbenches are out of scope for this strategy but named as adopters).\n54\t- Align the delivery target semantics with the cluster audit sink (the\n55\t  standard cluster logging forwarder interface) so events defined by this\n56\t  schema can be forwarded without translation.\n57\t\n58\t## Acceptance Criteria\n59\t\n60\t- The schema document exists and defines fields covering caller identity,\n61\t  model/version, timestamp, and response status.\n62\t- The schema states that payloads are excluded from audit events.\n63\t- The schema carries a version identifier and a documented compatibility\n64\t  expectation for future emitting components.\n65\t- The schema is published at an agreed, discoverable location reachable by\n66\t  platform components and by consumers of the cluster audit sink.\n67\t- The schema has been reviewed for component-agnosticism: nothing in it\n68\t  presumes a single emitter's internal structure.\n69\t\n70\t## HLR Traceability\n71\t\n72\t- HLR-4 (P2): A common audit event schema is defined and published so\n73\t  future components emit compatible events. This epic also produces the\n74\t  format that the P0 inference audit events (HLR-1) must conform to, which\n75\t  is why this epic is prioritized P0 despite HLR-4's P2 label.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f3c1eb39b6159b5","agent_id":"a87686f1509452071","session_id":"d259959c-9f88-4e8b-bf9c-c556eba940a6","uuid":"cf2f681e-86b1-4256-899c-0c7fbe98c733","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:53:54.347Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aaa034b8389fc4b5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9010 decomposition"}
