{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4bd440220e0351f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9010 (Audit logging for model inference traffic)\n10\t\n11\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Critical. The strategy defines\n12\tinference audit event emission in KServe, reliable delivery to the cluster\n13\taudit sink, a per-project on/off control in the Dashboard, a published common\n14\taudit event schema, and end-user documentation.\n15\t\n16\t**Triage:** Not below-threshold and not docs-only. Although the strategy is\n17\tsmall, it spans multiple architecturally distinct work streams (serving-side\n18\tevent emission and delivery pipeline, Dashboard UI control surface, and\n19\tsubstantive documentation authoring) that warrant separate PR review cycles\n20\tand different skills. Full decomposition follows.\n21\t\n22\t**Staff Engineer Input:** none present in the strategy.\n23\t\n24\t## Step 1 — Strategy structure\n25\t\n26\tThe strategy provides: High-Level Requirements (HLR-1..HLR-5 with\n27\tpriorities), Affected Components (KServe, Dashboard), Out of Scope, and Open\n28\tQuestions (\"None — the audit sink interface is the standard cluster logging\n29\tforwarder\"). Health warnings for missing sections are listed below.\n30\t\n31\t## Step 2 — Component graph\n32\t\n33\t| Component (canonical) | Change | Owner team | Classification |\n34\t|---|---|---|---|\n35\t| KServe | Emit audit events per inference request, deliver to cluster audit sink, honor per-project enablement | RHOAI Serving | Active |\n36\t| Dashboard | Administrator per-project enable/disable control for inference auditing | RHOAI Dashboard | Active |\n37\t| Documentation | New documentation topic: enabling auditing and interpreting events | RHOAI Documentation | Active (docs-authoring) |\n38\t\n39\tBoth KServe and Dashboard exist in `.context/architecture-context/`\n40\t(rhoai-3.0) and are Konflux-onboarded — no onboarding chain (Rule 20) is\n41\tneeded. No new components are provisioned.\n42\t\n43\tComponent mapping notes:\n44\t- The common audit event schema epic is assigned canonical component\n45\t  `KServe` (single most-relevant name): the schema is authored by the\n46\t  serving team whose component emits the first conforming events, and there\n47\t  is no canonical \"platform-wide schema\" component. The deliverable is\n48\t  explicitly component-agnostic per HLR-4.\n49\t- HLR-5 requires documentation, but `Documentation` is not listed in the\n50\t  strategy's Affected Components. It is added as an epic under the canonical\n51\t  `Documentation` component.\n52\t\n53\t## Step 3 — Investigations\n54\t\n55\tThe strategy declares no open questions, no pending reviews, and no\n56\tconditional ADRs; the audit sink interface is stated to be the standard\n57\tcluster logging forwarder. No unknown changes which epics exist or what they\n58\tdo. The only sequencing constraint (HLR-4's note that HLR-1 cannot ship\n59\tbefore the schema is settled) is a build-against artifact relationship\n60\t(Rule 13), not an uncertainty — it is modeled as a dependency edge, not an\n61\tInvestigation. The strategy is compliance-driven but is not a\n62\tguardrails/sandboxing/RBAC enforcement strategy (Rule 19 does not trigger).\n63\t\n64\t## Step 4 — HLR-to-epic mapping\n65\t\n66\t- HLR-1 (P0) and HLR-2 (P0) both describe the KServe audit event pipeline —\n67\t  emission at the inference endpoint and reliable at-least-once delivery of\n68\t  those same events. Same component, same team, same logical change over the\n69\t  same event stream: one Implementation epic covering both HLRs at P0.\n70\t- HLR-3 (P1) is a distinct, deferrable feature on a different component and\n71\t  UI surface (Dashboard per-project control): separate epic at P1.\n72\t- HLR-4 (P2) is the published common audit event schema. It is a distinct\n73\t  deliverable (a published contract for future components) that HLR-1's\n74\t  events must conform to, so it is its own epic. Because it transitively\n75\t  gates all P0 work (HLR-1/HLR-2), it inherits P0 (priority inheritance).\n76\t- HLR-5 (P2) is substantive documentation authoring (new doc topic), a\n77\t  separate work-product type: separate `docs-authoring` epic. Per the\n78\t  docs-authoring priority exception, its priority derives from the strategy\n79\t  frontmatter priority (Critical → P0), and its edges do not trigger\n80\t  priority inheritance.\n81\t\n82\t## Epic List\n83\t\n84\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n85\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n86\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 | Define and publish the common inference audit event schema | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n87\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 | KServe inference audit event emission and at-least-once delivery | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n88\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 | Dashboard per-project control for inference auditing | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n89\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 | Document enabling inference auditing and interpreting audit events | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P0 |\n90\t\n91\t## Dependency DAG\n92\t\n93\t```mermaid\n94\tgraph TD\n95\t    E001[\"E001 Common audit event schema\"] --> E002[\"E002 KServe emission + delivery\"]\n96\t    E002 --> E003[\"E003 Dashboard per-project control\"]\n97\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Documentation\"]\n98\t    E002 --> E004\n99\t    E003 --> E004\n100\t```\n101\t\n102\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E003 → E004 (length 4).\n103\t\n104\t## DAG Justification\n105\t\n106\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n107\t|---|---|---|\n108\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The schema is the format HLR-1's events must conform to; the strategy explicitly states HLR-1 cannot ship before the schema is settled. The emission implementation builds against the published schema. |\n109\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The Dashboard control writes the per-project audit enablement configuration that the serving-side implementation defines and honors. Per architecture context, the Dashboard owns no data plane and reads/writes CRs — it builds against the config surface produced by the KServe-side work. |\n110\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must describe the published schema as part of interpreting events. |\n111\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built (emission and delivery behavior). |\n112\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers enabling auditing per project from the Dashboard. |\n113\t\n114\tNotes:\n115\t- No `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics:\n116\t  both target components already exist in the platform and are Konflux\n117\t  onboarded per architecture context.\n118\t- No Investigation epics: no open questions, pending reviews, or conditional\n119\t  ADRs; the audit sink interface is stated as the standard cluster logging\n120\t  forwarder.\n121\t- docs-authoring edges (→ E004) do not trigger priority inheritance, so E003\n122\t  remains P1 despite blocking the P0 docs epic.\n123\t\n124\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n125\t\n126\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n127\t|---|---|---|\n128\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n129\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n130\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 |\n131\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 |\n132\t| HLR-5 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 |\n133\t\n134\tAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-3's serving-side enforcement\n135\t(the per-project enablement setting the serving stack honors) is scope\n136\twithin E002; its user-facing surface is E003.\n137\t\n138\t## Health Warnings\n139\t\n140\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** no Impacted Teams table, no\n141\t  Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n142\t  Non-Functional Requirements. Owner teams were derived from architecture\n143\t  context; epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n144\t- **Priority inversion:** HLR-4 is labeled P2 yet explicitly gates the P0\n145\t  HLR-1 (\"HLR-1 cannot ship before the schema is settled\"). Mitigated in the\n146\t  decomposition by priority inheritance: the schema epic (E001) is P0.\n147\t- **Scope trap (durable delivery):** HLR-2's \"not lost on component\n148\t  restart\" plus at-least-once semantics implies durable buffering in the\n149\t  serving delivery path — more than a simple logging hook. The strategy does\n150\t  not bound this; verify the intended durability mechanism during\n151\t  implementation planning.\n152\t- **Schema ownership tension:** HLR-4's schema must serve future components\n153\t  (pipelines, workbenches) but is being defined under the KServe component\n154\t  by the serving team. Reviewers should confirm the schema is genuinely\n155\t  component-agnostic and its publication location is agreed platform-wide.\n156\t- **Documentation component absent from Affected Components:** HLR-5\n157\t  requires documentation; the strategy lists only KServe and Dashboard. A\n158\t  Documentation epic was added — confirm the intended docs owner.\n159\t\n160\t## Tiered Delivery\n161\t\n162\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing; all work is\n163\tin-platform and linearly gated by the schema.\n164\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f3c1eb39b6159b5","agent_id":"a87686f1509452071","session_id":"d259959c-9f88-4e8b-bf9c-c556eba940a6","uuid":"8d46dcac-aad4-4f44-818c-d43af64af320","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:54:11.235Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4bd440220e0351f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9010 decomposition"}
