{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b94508b1363202f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9010 (Audit logging for model inference traffic)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Critical. The strategy defines\n13\tinference audit event emission in KServe, reliable delivery to the cluster\n14\taudit sink, a per-project on/off control in the Dashboard, a published common\n15\taudit event schema, and end-user documentation.\n16\t\n17\t**Triage:** Not below-threshold and not docs-only. Although the strategy is\n18\tsmall, it spans multiple architecturally distinct work streams (serving-side\n19\tevent emission and delivery pipeline, Dashboard UI control surface, and\n20\tsubstantive documentation authoring) that warrant separate PR review cycles\n21\tand different skills. Full decomposition follows.\n22\t\n23\t**Staff Engineer Input:** none present in the strategy.\n24\t\n25\t## Step 1 — Strategy structure\n26\t\n27\tThe strategy provides: High-Level Requirements (HLR-1..HLR-5 with\n28\tpriorities), Affected Components (KServe, Dashboard), Out of Scope, and Open\n29\tQuestions (\"None — the audit sink interface is the standard cluster logging\n30\tforwarder\"). Health warnings for missing sections are listed below.\n31\t\n32\t## Step 2 — Component graph\n33\t\n34\t| Component (canonical) | Change | Owner team | Classification |\n35\t|---|---|---|---|\n36\t| KServe | Emit audit events per inference request, deliver to cluster audit sink, honor per-project enablement | RHOAI Serving | Active |\n37\t| Dashboard | Administrator per-project enable/disable control for inference auditing | RHOAI Dashboard | Active |\n38\t| Documentation | New documentation topic: enabling auditing and interpreting events | RHOAI Documentation | Active (docs-authoring) |\n39\t\n40\tBoth KServe and Dashboard exist in `.context/architecture-context/`\n41\t(rhoai-3.0) and are Konflux-onboarded — no onboarding chain (Rule 20) is\n42\tneeded. No new components are provisioned.\n43\t\n44\tComponent mapping notes:\n45\t- The common audit event schema epic is assigned canonical component\n46\t  `KServe` (single most-relevant name): the schema is authored by the\n47\t  serving team whose component emits the first conforming events, and there\n48\t  is no canonical \"platform-wide schema\" component. The deliverable is\n49\t  explicitly component-agnostic per HLR-4.\n50\t- HLR-5 requires documentation, but `Documentation` is not listed in the\n51\t  strategy's Affected Components. It is added as an epic under the canonical\n52\t  `Documentation` component.\n53\t\n54\t## Step 3 — Investigations\n55\t\n56\tThe strategy declares no open questions, no pending reviews, and no\n57\tconditional ADRs; the audit sink interface is stated to be the standard\n58\tcluster logging forwarder. No unknown changes which epics exist or what they\n59\tdo. The only sequencing constraint (HLR-4's note that HLR-1 cannot ship\n60\tbefore the schema is settled) is a build-against artifact relationship\n61\t(Rule 13), not an uncertainty — it is modeled as a dependency edge, not an\n62\tInvestigation. The strategy is compliance-driven but is not a\n63\tguardrails/sandboxing/RBAC enforcement strategy (Rule 19 does not trigger).\n64\t\n65\t## Step 4 — HLR-to-epic mapping\n66\t\n67\t- HLR-1 (P0) and HLR-2 (P0) both describe the KServe audit event pipeline —\n68\t  emission at the inference endpoint and reliable at-least-once delivery of\n69\t  those same events. Same component, same team, same logical change over the\n70\t  same event stream: one Implementation epic covering both HLRs at P0.\n71\t- HLR-3 (P1) is a distinct, deferrable feature on a different component and\n72\t  UI surface (Dashboard per-project control): separate epic at P1.\n73\t- HLR-4 (P2) is the published common audit event schema. It is a distinct\n74\t  deliverable (a published contract for future components) that HLR-1's\n75\t  events must conform to, so it is its own epic. Because it transitively\n76\t  gates all P0 work (HLR-1/HLR-2), it inherits P0 (priority inheritance).\n77\t- HLR-5 (P2) is substantive documentation authoring (new doc topic), a\n78\t  separate work-product type: separate `docs-authoring` epic. Per the\n79\t  docs-authoring priority exception, its priority derives from the strategy\n80\t  frontmatter priority (Critical → P0), and its edges do not trigger\n81\t  priority inheritance.\n82\t\n83\t## Epic List\n84\t\n85\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n86\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n87\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 | Define and publish the common inference audit event schema | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n88\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 | KServe inference audit event emission and at-least-once delivery | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n89\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 | Dashboard per-project control for inference auditing | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n90\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 | Document enabling inference auditing and interpreting audit events | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P0 |\n91\t\n92\t## Dependency DAG\n93\t\n94\t```mermaid\n95\tgraph TD\n96\t    E001[\"E001 Common audit event schema\"] --> E002[\"E002 KServe emission + delivery\"]\n97\t    E002 --> E003[\"E003 Dashboard per-project control\"]\n98\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Documentation\"]\n99\t    E002 --> E004\n100\t    E003 --> E004\n101\t```\n102\t\n103\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E003 → E004 (length 4).\n104\t\n105\t## DAG Justification\n106\t\n107\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n108\t|---|---|---|\n109\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The schema is the format HLR-1's events must conform to; the strategy explicitly states HLR-1 cannot ship before the schema is settled. The emission implementation builds against the published schema. |\n110\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The Dashboard control writes the per-project audit enablement configuration that the serving-side implementation defines and honors. Per architecture context, the Dashboard owns no data plane and reads/writes CRs — it builds against the config surface produced by the KServe-side work. |\n111\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must describe the published schema as part of interpreting events. |\n112\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built (emission and delivery behavior). |\n113\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers enabling auditing per project from the Dashboard. |\n114\t\n115\tNotes:\n116\t- No `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics:\n117\t  both target components already exist in the platform and are Konflux\n118\t  onboarded per architecture context.\n119\t- No Investigation epics: no open questions, pending reviews, or conditional\n120\t  ADRs; the audit sink interface is stated as the standard cluster logging\n121\t  forwarder.\n122\t- docs-authoring edges (→ E004) do not trigger priority inheritance, so E003\n123\t  remains P1 despite blocking the P0 docs epic.\n124\t\n125\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n126\t\n127\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n128\t|---|---|---|\n129\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n130\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n131\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 |\n132\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 |\n133\t| HLR-5 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 |\n134\t\n135\tAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-3's serving-side enforcement\n136\t(the per-project enablement setting the serving stack honors) is scope\n137\twithin E002; its user-facing surface is E003.\n138\t\n139\t## Health Warnings\n140\t\n141\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** no Impacted Teams table, no\n142\t  Dependencies 