{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9beea54c5e763400","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9010-E002\n3\ttitle: KServe inference audit event emission and at-least-once delivery\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n5\tcomponent: KServe\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-9010-E001\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 0\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 1\n17\t  external_dependency: 1\n18\t  human_process_gates: 1\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 7\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# KServe inference audit event emission and at-least-once delivery\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic delivers inference audit logging in the KServe serving stack:\n35\tevery inference request processed through a KServe endpoint emits a\n36\tstructured audit event conforming to the published common audit event\n37\tschema, and events are delivered to the cluster audit sink (the standard\n38\tcluster logging forwarder interface) with at-least-once semantics that\n39\tsurvive component restarts. It also implements the serving-side honoring of\n40\tthe per-project inference auditing enablement setting that the Dashboard\n41\tcontrol writes.\n42\t\n43\t## Scope\n44\t\n45\t- Emit one audit event per inference request handled by a KServe endpoint,\n46\t  capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status,\n47\t  and never capturing request or response payloads.\n48\t- Emit events that conform to the published common inference audit event\n49\t  schema.\n50\t- Deliver events to the cluster audit sink via the standard cluster logging\n51\t  forwarder interface with at-least-once semantics.\n52\t- Ensure no audit events are lost when a serving component restarts\n53\t  (durable buffering or an equivalent mechanism in the delivery path, sized\n54\t  and bounded during implementation).\n55\t- Honor a per-project inference auditing enablement setting: audit event\n56\t  emission for a project's inference endpoints is gated by that project's\n57\t  setting, so administrators can enable/disable auditing per project.\n58\t- Cover all inference traffic through KServe endpoints in both deployment\n59\t  modes supported by the serving stack (serverless and raw deployment).\n60\t\n61\t## Acceptance Criteria\n62\t\n63\t- For a sample of inference requests through a KServe endpoint, each\n64\t  request produces exactly one audit event containing caller identity,\n65\t  model/version, timestamp, and response status, and containing no request\n66\t  or response payload data.\n67\t- Emitted events validate against the published common audit event schema.\n68\t- With the cluster logging forwarder configured as audit sink, events\n69\t  arrive at the sink at least once per emitted event.\n70\t- Restarting the serving component during active inference traffic does not\n71\t  result in loss of audit events for requests processed before the restart.\n72\t- With per-project auditing disabled for a project, inference requests to\n73\t  that project's endpoints produce no audit events; with it enabled, they\n74\t  produce events per the criteria above.\n75\t- The change is covered by the existing build pipeline for the serving\n76\t  stack (pipeline green on the merged change).\n77\t\n78\t## HLR Traceability\n79\t\n80\t- HLR-1 (P0): Every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an\n81\t  audit event capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and\n82\t  response status (not payloads).\n83\t- HLR-2 (P0): Audit events are delivered to the cluster audit sink with\n84\t  at-least-once semantics and are not lost on component restart.\n85\t- HLR-3 (P1, serving-side scope only): the per-project enablement setting\n86\t  honored here is the configuration surface that the Dashboard control\n87\t  writes; the user-facing Dashboard surface is tracked separately.\n88\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb49d0648fc7d8d0","agent_id":"a329e05d1a2384739","session_id":"d259959c-9f88-4e8b-bf9c-c556eba940a6","uuid":"5be77fe0-5a56-40ac-a512-6cde17a0ba86","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:56:10.404Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9beea54c5e763400","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9010 decomposition"}
